WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:05.670
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.507
Thanks for joining.

00:00:06.507 --> 00:00:08.340
My name is Stephanie
Wong, customer engineer

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.170
here, at Google.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.992
And we have here, of
course, you know--

00:00:10.992 --> 00:00:12.200
JONATHAN CHAM: Jonathan Cham.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.860
STEPHANIE WONG: And welcome to
GCP Online Meetup number 38.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:16.610
JONATHAN CHAM: 38!

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:17.443
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes!

00:00:17.443 --> 00:00:18.620
JONATHAN CHAM: Yes!

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.453
STEPHANIE WONG: So we're
super excited today

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:24.230
because we have Addison
Howard over here from Kaggle.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.090
And he is a program
manager, so he's

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.590
going to be talking
about everything

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:30.340
he knows about Kaggle, the
community involved in it,

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.920
and machine learning as well.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.730
So I'm going to let him take
it away and introduce himself.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:36.694
JONATHAN CHAM: Yes,
Addison, are you alive?

00:00:36.694 --> 00:00:37.610
ADDISON HOWARD: Hello.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:38.110
I am alive.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:38.901
JONATHAN CHAM: Yes.

00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:40.040
Alive and well.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.895
ADDISON HOWARD: Live
from Denver, Colorado.

00:00:42.895 --> 00:00:44.270
I'm go glad to be
a part of this.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.072
I'm really excited.

00:00:45.072 --> 00:00:46.780
Like Steph mentioned,
I'm Addison Howard.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.620
I'm a program manager on Kaggle.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.100
Kaggle's been a part of Google
now for about six months.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.177
We are the home for data
science and I'm super

00:00:55.177 --> 00:00:56.343
pumped to be a part of this.

00:00:56.343 --> 00:00:57.176
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah.

00:00:57.176 --> 00:00:58.190
Thanks, Addison.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.510
So, I mean, I don't know.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.462
Stephanie, when the topic came
up, will they take our jobs?

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:05.420
I was a little concerned,
because I don't know.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.225
Do you think our
jobs will be lost?

00:01:07.225 --> 00:01:07.550
STEPHANIE WONG: I don't know.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.383
I feel like I would be
pretty OK with having

00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:11.590
half my job be covered
by an automated service.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:12.800
I could put my feet up.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.370
JONATHAN CHAM: That's true.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:14.170
STEPHANIE WONG: Work part time.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.410
JONATHAN CHAM: And part
time sounds pretty good.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:16.326
It sounds pretty good.

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:18.760
But I mean, in
all seriousness, I

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.480
think the topic of automation,
and labor, and the economy,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.150
and AI, and ML-- like how
all these things fit together

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.030
and the impact of it
is a serious concern.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.270
And I mean, even politicians
are talking about it right now.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.240
So I thought it was a
good topic to bring up.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.170
And I know, Addison--

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.840
he's been dealing with
a lot of competitions,

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.480
a lot of work with
large companies

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.020
and small companies around
predictions, automations.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.600
And I'm guessing, if
anybody knows the answer

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.420
to this, Addison, probably,
has at least the best idea

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.415
about what he sees
in the future.

00:01:50.415 --> 00:01:51.290
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.575
Well, we'll see,
because I would say

00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:53.600
that the answer to
the question of,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.100
will machine learning
take your job?

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.209
Is kind of a big, fat maybe.

00:01:57.209 --> 00:01:59.000
I mean, when it comes
to the future of work

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.340
though, almost one
in every two jobs

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.654
do have a high risk of
being automated by machines.

00:02:04.654 --> 00:02:07.070
Now when you say that, I always
think of like "Terminator"

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.050
or something like
that, but, no, it's

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:13.290
things that can be automated and
repeated over and over again.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.910
But, on the flip side, machines
cannot do things that require

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.310
a novel approach or are
based on something that they

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.140
haven't seen many,
many times before.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.115
So when you think
about it, I always

00:02:24.115 --> 00:02:25.490
kind of find it
fascinating thing

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.680
that the term "data
science" itself didn't even

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.460
exist until 2008.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.850
Gosh, I was a junior in
college, at the time,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.327
and data science finally
came around as a term.

00:02:37.327 --> 00:02:39.410
And it's really accelerated
in the past few years.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.120
But you've already seen
it in some common things

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:41.840
in the world.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.712
So think about
mail-- snail mail.

00:02:45.712 --> 00:02:47.170
Back in the day,
somebody's job was

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.870
to look at every single
letter and say, well,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.745
I think that's a one,
I think that's a seven,

00:02:50.745 --> 00:02:51.650
and divide it up.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.690
Well, now they have
machine learning algorithms

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:56.280
that will scan handwritten
numbers and sort them.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.800
So that's an example
of something.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.860
But now you take that
to a newer extent

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.910
and you're seeing machines that
can read retina scans of eyes.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:10.480
And while one ophthalmologist
may look at a scan of an eye--

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.680
maybe 50,000 scans, over
the course of their career.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.160
We are now training a
machine to do the same thing

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:17.630
in a matter of hours.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:21.960
And so, overall, I will
say, it kind of depends.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.070
But ultimately, always be
reminded that machines cannot

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.852
do things that require a
novel approach or is based

00:03:27.852 --> 00:03:30.800
on something that they haven't
really seen many times before.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.450
STEPHANIE WONG: Maybe the
different way to look at it

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:33.908
is that machines
aren't necessarily

00:03:33.908 --> 00:03:34.932
replacing your jobs.

00:03:34.932 --> 00:03:37.140
I mean, they might be
replacing portions of your job,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.848
but it might be actually
saving you time,

00:03:38.848 --> 00:03:41.180
so that you can be doing
more interesting work instead

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.580
of manual, repetitive tasks.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:43.580
ADDISON HOWARD: Exactly.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.240
In the example of medicine,
we're now freeing up the time

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.730
that many ophthalmologists
have from looking

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.620
at scans over and
over again, to, now,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.060
being able to do more research
and do more ground breaking

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.339
things in the world.

00:03:58.339 --> 00:04:00.130
JONATHAN CHAM: So I
see, with my customers,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.005
that's exactly right.

00:04:01.005 --> 00:04:06.390
Taking over the entire job or
entire role is pretty rare.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:13.920
It's more about really helping
workers be more productive.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.863
I mean, we still need
mailmen, but instead

00:04:15.863 --> 00:04:17.779
of looking at numbers
and trying to figure out

00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:20.600
what zip code it is,
they're actually putting

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.324
mail inside of your mailbox.

00:04:22.324 --> 00:04:23.240
ADDISON HOWARD: Right.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.950
Or they're looking
at other ways to be

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:26.908
more efficient with their
shipping, and routes,

00:04:26.908 --> 00:04:28.250
and things like that.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.290
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.224
JONATHAN CHAM: Cool.

00:04:30.224 --> 00:04:31.140
So that's what I mean.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.681
I guess that's the
net of the answer.

00:04:32.681 --> 00:04:34.310
I'd love to hear from
the audience what

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:38.450
your thoughts are on this--

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.370
have you seen an
impact yourself?

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:45.110
What are your thoughts, in terms
of the impact on the economy?

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.249
I'd love to hear
what you guys think.

00:04:47.249 --> 00:04:49.790
But I mean, Addison, I know you
had a couple other things you

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:50.665
wanted to go through.

00:04:50.665 --> 00:04:51.794
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:04:51.794 --> 00:04:53.210
JONATHAN CHAM:
What does Kaggle do

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.750
and how has it impacted
the industry, as a whole?

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:58.125
Because I feel like it has.

00:04:58.125 --> 00:04:59.000
ADDISON HOWARD: Sure.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.239
So Kaggle is known as
the home of data science.

00:05:03.239 --> 00:05:05.280
The fun of fact is the
term Kaggle is, literally,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.940
just a amalgamation
of sounds that didn't

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.210
have a domain name taken yet.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.890
And that's how
Kaggle came to be.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.680
There's some fun,
internal, team folklore

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.620
around what the other
alternative would have them,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.909
but it's basically become
the home of data science.

00:05:21.909 --> 00:05:23.700
Originally, we kind of
came into the market

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.550
about six or seven years
ago, running machine learning

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:30.270
competitions, where our
CEO, Anthony Goldbloom,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.886
wanted to predict the
results of Eurovision.

00:05:32.886 --> 00:05:34.260
Are you familiar
with Eurovision?

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.450
It's the kind of the big,
"American Idol" style

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:40.920
competition, where
every country in Europe

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.496
sends a representative.

00:05:42.496 --> 00:05:44.370
And there's sort of some
alliances and things

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:45.515
like that occur.

00:05:45.515 --> 00:05:47.640
Well, they wanted to host
a competition to do that.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.710
And that quickly
expanded to people

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.620
coming to Kaggle to learn
how to do data science.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.230
Again, data science hasn't
been around for, really,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.550
much more than a decade.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.819
And a lot of people who
are currently in, usually,

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:01.860
the engineering community,
maybe computer science

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.470
community, maybe
mathematical community,

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.275
are wanting to get
involved in this.

00:06:06.275 --> 00:06:08.400
And there isn't a whole
lot of research out there--

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.990
or there wasn't at the time.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.930
And like many of us can likely
relate to, one of the best ways

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.100
to learn is to go figure
it out and try it out.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.570
And so through competitions,
what ended up happening

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:22.620
is four people interested in
data science on the community

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:23.620
side--

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.950
people will come and have
a really unique problem

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.530
they can then go test
their knowledge on.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.967
I'm trying to predict X,
I'm trying to guess Y--

00:06:31.967 --> 00:06:34.050
we'll go through a couple
of those here, in a bit.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.216
On the business side of the
house, what we're seeing

00:06:36.216 --> 00:06:37.896
is a lot of companies.

00:06:37.896 --> 00:06:39.770
They don't have a big
data science group yet.

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.490
Maybe they have
one or two folks,

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:42.540
maybe they don't
have some, maybe they

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.640
do have a couple of dozen
people who are data scientists.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.020
And they're saying,
we've been trying

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.561
to solve a couple
of problems, but we

00:06:47.561 --> 00:06:51.300
need some help, we need to then
through it over to a community.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:52.710
And Kaggle now has--

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.785
we think we've just
surpassed 1.3 million people

00:06:55.785 --> 00:06:58.410
in our community, who have come
to Kaggle saying, we want to do

00:06:58.410 --> 00:06:59.737
more, we want to learn more.

00:06:59.737 --> 00:07:00.570
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.445
ADDISON HOWARD: And
since competitions, we've

00:07:02.445 --> 00:07:04.950
now started adding in things
like datasets and kernels.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.646
Datasets being-- you always
hear about 80% of the work

00:07:07.646 --> 00:07:09.750
is just cleaning up and
munging data together,

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:12.550
this now provides a wide
variety of data for people

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:14.916
to look at, and play
around with, and analyze.

00:07:14.916 --> 00:07:17.290
And then kernels-- which we
can get into later, as well--

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:18.955
is a bit of code
sharing platform.

00:07:18.955 --> 00:07:19.830
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:21.660
I think one of the
daunting things about it

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.701
is trying to do something
by yourself, especially

00:07:23.701 --> 00:07:25.800
when it comes data
science, if you have little

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:26.510
to no experience.

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:29.790
So just having that
community forum and people

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:32.790
to validate your creations
with is something

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.960
that we haven't really seen in
the industry, like you said,

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.680
a decade before or even
just the past few years.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.284
JONATHAN CHAM: So outside of
the obvious reason why a company

00:07:43.284 --> 00:07:44.700
or organization
would use Kaggle--

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.830
and the obvious reason
would be like, hey,

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.130
I want to hedge my bet on
instead of hiring a bunch of ML

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.950
data scientists, I want to
see if this is even plausible

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.070
or feasible, let me
put out an aware--

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.894
like $10,000-- see what
the results look like.

00:07:58.894 --> 00:08:00.810
I mean, I feel like
that's the main reason why

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:01.559
people use Kaggle.

00:08:01.559 --> 00:08:04.140
Are there any other
reasons, outside of that,

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:07.485
Kaggle would be useful
for, for an organization?

00:08:07.485 --> 00:08:08.360
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.690
So two to come to mind--

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.251
one is a fresh perspective.

00:08:13.251 --> 00:08:15.750
So for example, one competition
that we're currently running

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:16.850
is Zillow--

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:17.910
The Zillow competition.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.930
If you're familiar with Zillow,
they have the Zestimate.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:24.970
And some people kind of maybe
complained, saying, oh, well,

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:26.690
that's not how much
my house is worth.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:27.970
JONATHAN CHAM: I'm just
saying, that's definitely

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:28.790
wrong for my house.

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:30.774
It's way too low.

00:08:30.774 --> 00:08:32.940
ADDISON HOWARD: And Zillow
is saying, yeah, we know,

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.250
we get it.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:36.049
We understand it's
not always correct

00:08:36.049 --> 00:08:37.120
and we want to do better.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:38.940
And so they have a
team of data scientist

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:41.424
that have been working on this
algorithm and working on it.

00:08:41.424 --> 00:08:42.840
And there are some
things that are

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.812
more technical around
overfitting or tweaking

00:08:44.812 --> 00:08:46.770
the same thing until the
point where it doesn't

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:48.620
become very useful anymore.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:51.480
They said, we have a
big team, but let's

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.142
get some fresh eyes
on this and see,

00:08:53.142 --> 00:08:54.600
is there more meat
left on the bone

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.350
to get some value out of this
algorithm we're creating?

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:58.670
I think that's one common area.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:00.899
And a second area
is novel approaches.

00:09:00.899 --> 00:09:02.940
For those who are familiar
with machine learning,

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.530
there's sort of been
different trends

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.170
across the industry around
what techniques are useful.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.100
So a big, popular
one in the beginning

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:10.770
was called Random Forest.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.110
But there's other ones that
come up throughout the years,

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.590
but Kaggle's kind
of been the place

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:18.390
where those have been elevated.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:21.480
And so one of the really big,
common ones now is XGBoost.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:22.350
JONATHAN CHAM: I was just
going to mention that.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:22.440
ADDISON HOWARD: Exactly.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:23.280
JONATHAN CHAM: I
don't know what it is,

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:24.410
but I hear it everywhere.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:25.410
ADDISON HOWARD: Exactly.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:26.550
And that's something that
has really kind of come

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.770
to the forefront
as being something

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:30.480
that's useful, through Kaggle.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.130
Because, like you mentioned,
it's such a big community.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:34.838
Instead of it just
being somebody holding

00:09:34.838 --> 00:09:37.990
onto their methodology, they're
still win the competition,

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.750
but then they'll say, hey,
guys, here's what I did.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:43.126
Data scientists,
here's my methodology.

00:09:43.126 --> 00:09:44.250
I tried this new technique.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.180
Why don't you guys
play around with it

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.980
and see what you can
do with it as well?

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.140
STEPHANIE WONG: What has the
community generally looked at,

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:51.300
from what you've seen, in
terms of how teams are formed

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.466
and the people that are
really interested in getting

00:09:53.466 --> 00:09:54.540
involved in contests?

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:55.410
Are they students?

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:56.369
Are they professionals?

00:09:56.369 --> 00:09:57.243
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:09:57.243 --> 00:09:58.560
It's kind of a mix of both.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.200
One cool thing that
we've recently released

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.880
is, actually, a
university-style platform,

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.880
like university method, where
a lot of teachers are saying,

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.550
we could really teach data
science well in our classroom

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.500
with a competition type format.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.262
And so we have
something called InClass

00:10:13.262 --> 00:10:15.220
that is, pretty much,
just for students to have

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:18.170
that type of learning ground.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:20.162
But on the same
side, it's really

00:10:20.162 --> 00:10:22.620
a wide variety of people from
all types of backgrounds that

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:23.786
come to be a part of Kaggle.

00:10:23.786 --> 00:10:26.260
And so what we see is,
as competitions start,

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:26.950
people start--

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:29.033
discussion forums are very,
very prevalent for us.

00:10:29.033 --> 00:10:30.840
And people start
flooding them with, hey,

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.280
I found these three articles
that might be helpful.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.020
Hey, this is my professional,
educational background,

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:39.370
anybody want to be on my team?

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:41.203
And we'll literally see
people saying, sure,

00:10:41.203 --> 00:10:43.450
message me, I'm in Germany.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:44.650
I'm in Bangladesh.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:45.700
I'm in Hawaii.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:47.030
Let's create a team together.

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:49.000
And it's cool to see
people who have never

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.300
met each other in person--

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.270
and sometimes they will
win the competition,

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:55.550
and when I introduce them
to one of these clients,

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:58.245
they're meeting for the
first time, via video call.

00:10:58.245 --> 00:11:00.370
Because they're just saying,
hey, I'm here to learn

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:02.620
and I want to be able to
learn from anybody, no matter

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:05.307
where they are, skill-wise
or geographically.

00:11:05.307 --> 00:11:06.140
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:08.980
I know some companies are using
it as a recruiting platform,

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:09.800
actually.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.511
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:11:11.511 --> 00:11:13.631
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah.

00:11:13.631 --> 00:11:15.380
ADDISON HOWARD: It's
actually pretty neat.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.470
I mean, a lot of the
big players have--

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:20.592
I say big players because
a couple of them--

00:11:20.592 --> 00:11:22.300
they were pre-acquisition
and maybe there

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:25.801
might be some brands that
would be rivals to us.

00:11:25.801 --> 00:11:26.300
But no.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.000
So you can look at our website.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:29.030
You see Walmarts, and
Facebooks, and All States

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:31.570
have come to us on a very
regular basis, saying,

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:35.500
this is a great
way for us to have

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.775
almost like an
accelerated job interview.

00:11:37.775 --> 00:11:39.150
Instead of putting
them in a room

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.780
and making them do
a quick hackathon,

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:42.210
let's give them three
months to go figure

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:43.630
out what they can
go do, and give them

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:44.800
a problem similar
to what they would

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.900
do on a regular basis
that can attract people

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:50.360
who are interested, and
give them a day in the life.

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:54.290
STEPHANIE WONG: Cool.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:56.950
I actually have a
question from online.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.380
Do you know how community
engagement is encouraged?

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:00.800
I mean, besides
just, organically,

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:04.130
how people comment and want
to be involved in a project.

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:05.630
But are there other
interesting ways

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:08.060
that you've tried to
encourage different community

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:11.540
topics to be discussed
or people to start voting

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:13.255
on different solutions?

00:12:13.255 --> 00:12:14.130
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:18.290
So I will say, a majority
of it is organic.

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.000
For some people,
they're incentivized

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.830
by that we actually
have rankings.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:24.020
So we have rankings to say
how good of a competitor

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.710
are you, how good of a kernel
author, code sharing person

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:30.792
are you, and how good of a
discussion person are you.

00:12:30.792 --> 00:12:32.500
And you can get
different rates based off

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.870
of expert, master, grand master.

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:38.060
We have occasional
private competitions, just

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.460
for our masters
and grandmasters.

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.280
And I think a lot of it is
that people just, genuinely,

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:45.500
come to learn and people,
genuinely, come to--

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:46.890
hey, I can share.

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.760
If I share my knowledge
here, then somebody else

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.520
can maybe glean off
that and expand upon it.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.380
And so we've,
occasionally, actually

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:56.900
offered some prizes
for best kernels,

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:00.200
best new datasets to
continue that engagement.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:03.110
But I've been very surprised
and very pleased at,

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:06.272
really, how organic
that has been, as it is.

00:13:06.272 --> 00:13:07.730
STEPHANIE WONG:
You were telling us

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:09.530
earlier about the
Twitter conversations

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:11.640
that you have right now
about machine learning.

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:11.960
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:13.725
STEPHANIE WONG: So can you
talk about that a little bit?

00:13:13.725 --> 00:13:15.140
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:17.510
It's been pretty neat.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.029
A full team of ours, at Kaggle,
is just a community team

00:13:20.029 --> 00:13:22.070
that's looking for ways
to continue to get people

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:25.610
involved, both inside
competitions-- which, again,

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:28.520
what we kind of had been
known, historically, but now,

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:31.010
as we've expanded to become
truly a broader home for data

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:32.070
science--

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:35.050
and so the past three weeks--

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.740
and we have one
coming up next week.

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:38.210
I'm actually not sure how
many more we after that,

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:39.251
coming into the holidays.

00:13:39.251 --> 00:13:42.260
But we've been hosting
debates on Twitter

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:44.900
that have been posing a
certain problem or a question

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:48.570
within data science and
saying, let's talk about this.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:52.180
And so the very first one
we had was with, actually,

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:56.660
a guy that used to be a Kaggle
grand master discussion expert.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:59.900
And then we, basically, poached
him from our own community

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.310
and brought him on board.

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:04.192
And was answering the question,
OK, AI, what's the hype?

00:14:04.192 --> 00:14:05.150
Where's that over-hype?

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:06.108
Where's the under-hype?

00:14:06.108 --> 00:14:07.695
What can it really accomplish?

00:14:07.695 --> 00:14:10.070
Last week, we had another one
with our committee members,

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:12.804
talking about bias and
biased machine learning.

00:14:12.804 --> 00:14:14.720
One example, if you're
not familiar with that,

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.570
would be if I were to
create an algorithm that's

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:21.260
trying to take stock
photo pictures of people

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:24.601
in professions and say, OK,
is this a doctor or a lawyer,

00:14:24.601 --> 00:14:26.850
but if every picture of a
doctor that I bring in there

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:28.520
is an old white guy,
then, all of a sudden,

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:30.605
the algorithms only think
that doctors are all old,

00:14:30.605 --> 00:14:31.010
white guys.

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:32.010
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:34.551
ADDISON HOWARD: And so there's
a lot of questions around bias

00:14:34.551 --> 00:14:35.600
that's been recurring.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:38.750
And one that just happened last
night with one of our community

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:40.920
leads was, which
is more important--

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:43.040
the algorithm or the data?

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:45.260
Is having a good
algorithm, but bad data,

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:47.960
or vise versa going to get
you better or worse results?

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:49.335
And we were talking
through that.

00:14:49.335 --> 00:14:51.930
And we've been hosting
those every Tuesday.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:53.230
You can follow the hashtag--

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:54.050
#MLDebate.

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:55.240
I believe that's what it is.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:56.330
And you can check out
our Twitter account

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:57.200
and see that as well.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:58.033
JONATHAN CHAM: Cool.

00:14:58.033 --> 00:14:59.030
Yeah.

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:01.790
I forgot what podcast, but
I was listening to something

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:05.480
about how sometimes
ML can perpetuate

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:06.714
negative stereotypes.

00:15:06.714 --> 00:15:07.630
ADDISON HOWARD: Right.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:08.505
JONATHAN CHAM: Right.

00:15:08.505 --> 00:15:10.890
And sometimes it's
very controversial.

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:14.157
So it's just really interesting.

00:15:14.157 --> 00:15:16.490
ADDISON HOWARD: And to kind
of elaborate on that, what's

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:18.620
interesting about those
problems is sometimes

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.930
you just don't have
you the capability

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:22.620
to get all the
[INAUDIBLE] you want.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:25.130
Especially if you're
starting working with humans,

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.910
it's hard to get a very
true, representative sample.

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:31.529
So we need to often be conscious
that machine learning isn't

00:15:31.529 --> 00:15:33.320
always designed to be
the perfect solution,

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:36.440
but maybe creating an algorithm
is to go from step zero

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:39.232
to eight and then maybe
take the rest by getting it

00:15:39.232 --> 00:15:40.790
from eight to 10.

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:43.040
JONATHAN CHAM: So we do have
a question from the chat.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:45.620
Can you cover how someone
with no ML experience

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:46.737
could use Kaggle?

00:15:46.737 --> 00:15:49.070
I'll answer that from my
perspective and, Addison, maybe

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:50.300
I'll let you answer--

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:51.950
maybe Stephanie.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:54.590
So I actually had
no ML experience

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:57.770
when Google acquired
Kaggle, so I went to Kaggle.

00:15:57.770 --> 00:15:59.250
First of all, what is Kaggle?

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.920
And then I noticed
that, OK, it's bunch

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.350
of competitions of something.

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:05.250
But then I got really
interested in it.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.657
Basically, there's
a problem statement

00:16:07.657 --> 00:16:08.990
and you have to solve it, right?

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:09.230
ADDISON HOWARD: Right.

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:11.780
JONATHAN CHAM: So what I did was
I went into these competitions.

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:13.940
I started looking at all these
things called kernels, which

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:16.160
are kind of like open
notebooks that people write in

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:19.010
to develop some kind of
algorithm or software,

00:16:19.010 --> 00:16:22.040
to help, basically,
take the dataset that's

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:25.820
provided by the customer and
do some manipulation, modeling,

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.020
and training on it.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:30.440
And the ability to look
through, basically,

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:34.670
hundreds of peoples' notebooks
and look at how they take

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.750
a perspective on the problem--

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.010
that's how I learned about ML.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.730
I'm definitely not the
expert, but it was just

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:45.710
a wealth of knowledge sitting
in all these Jupyter Notebooks.

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:46.831
So maybe I'll have--

00:16:46.831 --> 00:16:47.330
Stephanie?

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:48.910
And then I'll let
Addison answer that.

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:49.100
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:50.016
No, I agree with that.

00:16:50.016 --> 00:16:52.950
I mean, I think I'm also getting
started with learning much

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:54.570
more about machine
learning and how

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:57.060
to leverage a lot of the
tools that people are using.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.250
And being able to see
and learn from example

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:02.024
is key, as you said,
because it just difficult

00:17:02.024 --> 00:17:02.940
to start with nothing.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.619
And I don't even know where
to get started sometimes.

00:17:04.619 --> 00:17:06.240
A lot of it can
go over your head.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.130
So having those example
datasets and what

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:10.170
people have thought
of, out of the box,

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:11.940
is just incredibly helpful.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.430
And I'm sure Addison will
agree with that as well.

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:16.055
JONATHAN CHAM: And
just one more note--

00:17:16.055 --> 00:17:18.630
the top rated kernels
usually have very, very, very

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:21.150
good documentation, meaning
that the person took the time

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:24.464
to write out exactly what
that person did and why.

00:17:24.464 --> 00:17:26.130
And so, I mean, just
reading all of that

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.379
is a good learning experience.

00:17:27.379 --> 00:17:30.150
And there's a lot of comments,
a lot of community engagements,

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.792
which is why I think Kaggle's
a great place to learn ML.

00:17:32.792 --> 00:17:34.500
So if you don't know
ML, actually, Kaggle

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:35.540
is the place to go.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:35.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:36.080
ADDISON HOWARD: Right.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.170
JONATHAN CHAM: And if
you're awesome at ML,

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:38.254
it's also the place to go.

00:17:38.254 --> 00:17:39.128
ADDISON HOWARD: Yeah.

00:17:39.128 --> 00:17:40.020
I think you're right.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:41.290
And you said it well.

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.290
I think it provides a lot
of opportunities based

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:45.296
on whatever level you're at.

00:17:45.296 --> 00:17:47.670
I will say, just to play a
little bit of devil's advocate

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:51.969
against myself, if
you're coming in--

00:17:51.969 --> 00:17:54.260
so I don't have a computer
developer background at all.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:55.830
I'm more of a business guy.

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:59.350
And so I have experience in
math, econ, and that sort

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:00.240
of side of the house.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.790
So I'm starting right now
and teaching myself Python.

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:04.560
So I'm getting
down to that level.

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.000
But a lot of times
you might say, OK,

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:09.120
I have this technical
skill, but I have nowhere

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:10.707
to apply it or test it.

00:18:10.707 --> 00:18:13.040
We have a couple of different
introductory competitions,

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:14.670
as one way of doing that.

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:17.370
For example, the most popular
competition we've ever held

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:20.185
is one on the Titanic,
where we said, basically,

00:18:20.185 --> 00:18:21.810
is this person going
to survive or not?

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:23.180
And you give them a whole list.

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:25.620
And so you can think, just
on an intuitive level.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.870
Or what if you just
said, men don't survive,

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:29.500
women and children do.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:31.170
You could probably
get 80% right.

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:32.220
Then maybe you start
toggling things,

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:33.525
like, OK, what if
they were wealthy?

00:18:33.525 --> 00:18:34.570
What if they weren't?

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:35.790
What if they were on the
top part of the ship?

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:36.610
What if they weren't?

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:38.360
And that sort of gives
you the opportunity

00:18:38.360 --> 00:18:42.750
to play around with that and
a very free, low ego, low risk

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:44.019
environment.

00:18:44.019 --> 00:18:45.810
And we started having
more advanced things.

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:51.260
Can you predict housing prices,
in a non-Zillow sort of way?

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:53.010
If you want to get
more image recognition,

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:55.260
we have a competition for
that, but it's introductory.

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:57.060
So that's one of the best ways.

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:59.650
As Jonathan mentioned, kernels,
I think, are great as well.

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:02.250
Kernels, for us-- it's
not just code sharing,

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.560
but what we would
like to do is take out

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:06.340
a lot of the dependencies.

00:19:06.340 --> 00:19:09.300
So if someone were to share
you their code otherwise,

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:12.990
maybe you go download all 16
of these different libraries,

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:14.820
and versions, and
