00:26:22.070 --> 00:26:23.611
There's a lot of
companies that truly

00:26:23.611 --> 00:26:25.850
do see the value of
publicly available data

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:27.725
for broader analysis.

00:26:27.725 --> 00:26:30.400
And we're getting a lot
of that being brought

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:32.782
into our community as well.

00:26:32.782 --> 00:26:34.490
And I'll touch, very
briefly, on kernels.

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:35.906
We kind of mentioned
this as well,

00:26:35.906 --> 00:26:38.670
but this is a big opportunity
to share your own work.

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:41.240
And so, as I mentioned, the
biggest value that people see

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:42.210
is sort of twofold--

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:45.890
one is sharing, and
two is not having

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:49.100
to deal with downloading a
wide variety of libraries,

00:26:49.100 --> 00:26:51.660
and versions, and languages,
and things like that.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:55.200
What's very common is what
we call forking kernels.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:58.250
And so somebody might have a
particular script or notebook

00:26:58.250 --> 00:26:59.720
that they have,
but somebody else

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:03.300
wants to use it for
a different use case.

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:07.590
So for example, maybe somebody
has a McDonald's use case

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:09.860
and they did a Twitter
[INAUDIBLE] analysis

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:10.877
on McDonald's.

00:27:10.877 --> 00:27:12.710
And somebody else says,
this is a great code

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:13.501
that I want to use.

00:27:13.501 --> 00:27:15.334
I want to apply it
to United Airlines.

00:27:15.334 --> 00:27:17.750
And they then go in and then
do that with United Airlines.

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:19.250
They could then copy and paste--

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:20.570
or rather, we call it forking--

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:22.778
and then, basically, just
tweak that part of the code

00:27:22.778 --> 00:27:25.144
that they need to, to
shift that new data.

00:27:25.144 --> 00:27:26.060
And you can see here--

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:29.180
I mean, some of these have
hundreds of comments that

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:31.680
are people talking about it.

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:35.550
This top one is
recently exciting.

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:37.970
It's been updated in
the past two hours.

00:27:37.970 --> 00:27:40.150
Let's see if we can
get this loaded here,

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:42.350
in the last few
minutes that we've got.

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:48.140
As you mentioned, Jonathan,
it's very, very well documented,

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:51.320
so you see things
like people speaking

00:27:51.320 --> 00:27:54.020
in more of a common
language, all different types

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.390
of visualizations.

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:58.057
Usually, you can see
some fun animation.

00:27:58.057 --> 00:28:00.140
Sometimes people will bring
that in there as well.

00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:02.360
So it gives people a chance
to really learn and grow

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:04.640
from whatever stage
of development

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:06.900
that you're currently in
withe machine learning.

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:07.290
JONATHAN CHAM: Cool.

00:28:07.290 --> 00:28:08.289
